                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "I recommend Country Tales for Time Management enthusiasts who are familiar with the genre and already have a library of such titles, but further recommend waiting for it to go on sale. I don't recommend this to those less familiar with the Time Management genre, because of some weird design choices I will elaborate below. \n\nThe first glaring problem is that the tutorials that start many of the boards actually mislead the player into making the wrong choices early on if they want to meet the timed goals. I figured this out about 20% into the game. It was a eureka moment - Oh, I need to ignore the instructions because these tutorial characters are jerks! OK. So if she says \"Start by building a shack here.\" ignore her lying mouth. The funny thing is, this was finally documented at the beginning of level 40 (the very last board) on a loading screen tip I will quote: \"Tutorials are there to teach you how to play. They don't represent the best possible course of action.\" That's not what the tutorials do however. The teach you how to lose. It is like teaching someone to play chess by telling them to make sure they expose their king.\n\nThe next weird design choice is the leveling up of your workers by an experience system. This is not a strategic buff used at just the right moment, as with most Time Management games. Instead, after a certain number of boards the workers get faster / more productive. The idea is that you can then go back and replay earlier levels to get a better score. Now, it is one thing to do better on an earlier level with a different strategy or when you become a better player. It is quite another to simply go back and use exactly the same strategy but achieve better results because your workers move faster. That is just bizarre.\n\nThe final problem I have is with the way the game is advertised. First off, there are not \"Hundreds of Quests\". Build 5 villas is not a quest. It is what is known as a goal or objective. Second, these characters aren't \"peculiar\". They are stereotypes, and some are racist stereotypes at that. At one point the main character Woody ... I mean Ted ... is given tasks by a half naked child named Tiger Lily ... I mean Little Red Moon ... who, in spite of being sensitively referred to as a \"Native American\", is still a glaring example of the \"Indian Princess\" trope. Can developers *please* stop doing that?\n\nI hope this explains why I could not recommend the game in general, in spite of it being, in most respects, a fairly standard, functional Time Management title.",
